WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.060
Claude Code just fixed one bug…

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.030
and stopped. There's still one left.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.340
Open another agent, and normally, it starts from zero.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.450
You explain everything, again.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.270
With Continuum, it doesn't have to.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.270
Connect each agent once.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.650
One command each.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.620
While Claude works, Continuum records the task,

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.800
what's done, and what comes next.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.240
So when Codex starts, it's already briefed.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.480
What's done, what's left, and exactly what to do next.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.410
Codex picks up the same task, and fixes the second bug.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.580
Run the tests through Continuum, and the result is verified,

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.550
against the exact code it ran on.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.960
And in the dashboard, it's one task,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.210
carried from one agent to the next.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.590
Your work continues.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.080
Your agents change.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.440
Continuum. It's free, and it runs on your machine.
